From: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: offload towards ports of a 
 VLAN-aware bridge

The emulated head tag carries no VLAN (VSEL=0, KEEP=0), so the RTL8372N
classifies the frame to its CPU port's PVID, VLAN 1 on the rtl8372n
driver, and sends it out of the named port tagged or untagged as VLAN
1's entry says. The switch exempts the CPU's frames from its egress
membership check, so the driver checks it.

Besides the switch bridging the port itself (previous patch), require
its VLAN filtering on the port to equal the bridge's, the port to
forward, and, in a VLAN-aware 802.1Q bridge on an rtl8372n, either
nothing is pushed and the port's PVID is 1 and VLAN 1 is the only VLAN
it egresses untagged in, or VLAN 1 is pushed towards a tagged member of
VLAN 1. Every port's flows are dropped when its VLANs or STP state
change or when it joins or leaves a bridge or gets a VLAN upper, and
every port-addressed flow when a bridge's VLAN filtering or protocol
changes: synchronously in the notifier (before or after the bridge
applies the change, depending on the change; an STP state is notified
deferred, together with DSA's own port state update), and again from a
workqueue once it is complete; a generation count makes flows offered in
between retry (-EAGAIN, which withdraws nothing). The MTU of the
bridge's VLAN 1 upper caps the PMTU too. The FDB follower applies the
same conditions to the port it re-points a flow to and follows only
learns in VID 1 of a VLAN-aware bridge.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
